1.  Summarize the video presentation about the reproducibility crisis (**Step 1**).

The reproducibility crisis occurs when scientists cannot recreate past results using the same data and code. To solve this, the speaker recommends separating raw data, code, and results into a clear project structure. Other key practices include writing README files, using Git/GitHub for version control, automating workflows with scripts, applying literate coding, and archiving projects in repositories like OSF.

    1.  Summarize the new things you learned in this module about reproducible data science and literate coding.
    
    Literate coding weaves human-readable narratives with executable code in a single document. Quarto takes this further by effortlessly translating complex logic into clear reports. I also learned how to use terminal commands, like quarto publish, to instantly render .qmd files into HTML and deploy them directly to the web, ensuring transparent and shareable analysis workflows.
    
    2.  What do you think about the reproducible crisis in your field? How can Quarto help address the concerns you have about reproducibility in your line of work?
    
    In marketing data analysis, reproducibility issues arise from lost tracking pixel logic or attribution rules. Months later, predicting models become impossible to replicate. Quarto solves this by integrating R, Python, or SQL code directly with business logic documentation. This aligns code with commercial strategy, ensuring future reviews of predictive models or validation alerts are instantly understandable and reproducible.
    
